  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>A patient who is diabetic receives 100 micrograms of insulin. The graph shows the amount of insulin, in micrograms,
  remaining in his bloodstream over time, in minutes.</para>
      <para>
        
          <media alt="Graph of an exponential function, origin O. time (minutes) insulin (mg).">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_07_09_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>
        Scientists have found that the amount of insulin in a patient’s body changes exponentially. How can you
        check if the graph supports the scientists’ claim?
     </para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para/><para>Compare your answer:</para>
<para>If the insulin decays exponentially, that means that after each minute, the same fraction of insulin will decay and remain. So one way to check if this prediction matches the data is to see what fraction of insulin decays (or remains) after the first minute, then after the second minute, and so on. If these fractions are the same, then the graph supports the scientists’ claim.</para>

  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Answer the following questions:
     </para>
          <para>
          How much insulin broke down in the first minute?
       </para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para/><para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>10 micrograms broke down.</para>
    </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> What fraction of the original insulin is that?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para/><para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>  
    \(\frac{1}{10}\) of the initial injection</para>
  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Answer the following questions. 
     </para>
          <para>
          How much insulin broke down in the second minute?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para/><para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>9 micrograms broke down.</para>
    </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> What fraction is that of the amount one minute earlier?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para/><para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> \(\frac{1}{10}\) of the amount one minute earlier</para>
  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>
        What fraction of insulin remains in the bloodstream for each minute that passes? Be prepared to show your
        reasoning.
     </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\frac{9}{10}\). For example: In the next minute, another \(\frac{1}{10}\) of insulin broke down. Each time,
      \(\frac{9}{10}\) of the amount remains.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>
        Complete the table to show the predicted amount of insulin 4 and 5 minutes after injection.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
              <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Time after Injection (Minutes)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
            0
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            1
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            2
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            3
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            4
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            5
          </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Insulin in the Bloodstream (Micrograms)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
            100
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            90
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            81
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            72.9
          </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
   </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answers:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002">
              <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Time after Injection (Minutes)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          0
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          1
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          2
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          3
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          4
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          5
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Insulin in the Bloodstream (Micrograms)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          100
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          90
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          81
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          72.9
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          65.61
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          59.05
        </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Answer the following questions.
     </para>
          <para>
          Describe how you would find how many micrograms of insulin remain in his bloodstream after 10 minutes. 
       </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Multiply 100 by \(\frac{9}{10}\) ten times: \(100 \cdot (\frac{9}{10})^{10}\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>
            After \(m\) minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> For example: Multiply 100 by \(\frac{9}{10}\) \(m\) times: \(100 \cdot (\frac{9}{10})^m\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
